<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="DotNetCore.CAP" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing" />
    <!--
      Eventually.AssertAsync retries assertion-shaped failures only, which means it has to recognise
      Xunit.Sdk.XunitException, and TimerRegistrationObservingTimeProvider reports a lost fake-clock tick
      as one. That type lives in xunit.assert, so this references xunit.assert alone rather than the
      `xunit` metapackage (which would also drag in xunit.core and xunit.analyzers, neither of which this
      library uses). The sibling Nerv.IIP.Testing.Xunit is *not* a narrower home for it: it carries
      xunit.extensibility.core, whose xunit.core.dll does not contain XunitException at all, and moving
      Eventually.AssertAsync there would split it from the Eventually.WaitAsync it delegates to while
      leaving its three real consumers (Erp/Mes/Notification acceptance tests) unable to see it — they
      reference Nerv.IIP.Testing, not Nerv.IIP.Testing.Xunit.

      Boundary: 22 projects reference this library. 21 are xUnit test projects; the 22nd is
      Nerv.IIP.Testing.PostgreSql, a shared test-support library under common/Testing that is itself only
      consumed by test projects. No publishable/production assembly references any of them, which is the
      invariant that matters here — not "every consumer is a test project", which is not true.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.assert" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
